
the issue is that OS has to atomically decide if the page is dirty and then if dirty
queue for writeback.

trouble is after it checks for writeback more writes could come on the page and 
if you read it as readonly you would miss writing these back.

if you read back as dirty you could still lose data because while you are writing back
more data could be written to the page.



to prevent this pte_wrprotect is invoked to make sure page is write protected. this is 
not invoked directly rather its invoked via reverse mapping object service 
page_mkclean which does an atomic wrprotect and return previous state.

thats it!!

-----
summary look at 

clear_page_dirty_for_io (page-writeback.c)	
           if (page_mkclean(page))
                        set_page_dirty(page);

that in turn boils down to per pte_wrprotect using reverse mapping
